1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,278
Section four: seeds.

2
00:00:02,558 --> 00:00:04,926
Some data is not in any source system.

3
00:00:05,206 --> 00:00:09,067
It is a small list we maintain ourselves, like codes and their names.

4
00:00:09,347 --> 00:00:10,883
That is what seeds are for.

5
00:00:11,983 --> 00:00:13,797
The seeds page, version one.

6
00:00:14,077 --> 00:00:19,432
A seed is a CSV file in the project. dbt seed loads it as a table.

7
00:00:19,711 --> 00:00:23,872
Because it is a file in the project, it is version controlled and reviewed.

8
00:00:24,151 --> 00:00:27,223
Seeds are best for static data that changes rarely.

9
00:00:28,323 --> 00:00:33,806
Good seeds are small, static lists: code mappings, regions, test accounts to exclude.

10
00:00:34,086 --> 00:00:41,532
Bad seeds are raw data exported to CSV, anything large or sensitive, and anything that changes every day.

11
00:00:42,632 --> 00:00:43,976
We need three seeds.

12
00:00:44,256 --> 00:00:48,992
The official zone lookup from the Taxi Commission, with two hundred sixty-five zones.

13
00:00:49,272 --> 00:00:53,410
And two lists we create from the dictionary: payment types and rate codes.

14
00:00:53,690 --> 00:00:56,848
They turn numbers in the trip data into readable names.

15
00:00:57,948 --> 00:01:01,084
On the graph, three new nodes on the left, next to the source.

16
00:01:01,364 --> 00:01:04,436
Like sources, seeds are where data enters the project.

17
00:01:05,536 --> 00:01:09,098
The zone lookup is an official file, so nobody should retype it.

18
00:01:09,378 --> 00:01:14,562
In the demo, we download it from the Taxi Commission page and drop it into the seeds folder.

19
00:01:14,842 --> 00:01:17,744
Notice the column names are not snake case yet.

20
00:01:18,024 --> 00:01:19,496
We fix that in staging.

21
00:01:20,596 --> 00:01:31,092
The other two seeds, the agent creates from the dictionary file: every payment type, every rate code, a YAML file with descriptions, and integer types for the IDs.

22
00:01:31,372 --> 00:01:35,148
Then it asks to run dbt seed, and waits for our approval.

23
00:01:36,248 --> 00:01:37,101
The review.

24
00:01:37,381 --> 00:01:41,904
Payment types must start at zero, Flex Fare, and include six, voided trip.

25
00:01:42,184 --> 00:01:44,914
Rate codes must include ninety-nine, unknown.

26
00:01:45,194 --> 00:01:49,973
Older versions of the dictionary did not have these, so a list from memory would be wrong.

27
00:01:50,253 --> 00:01:53,197
We gave the agent the file, so it got the current list.

28
00:01:54,297 --> 00:01:56,302
Seeds get YAML just like models.

29
00:01:56,582 --> 00:02:01,638
Descriptions, and column types, so the IDs are integers instead of guessed types.

30
00:02:01,918 --> 00:02:04,670
Even a small lookup gets a primary key test.

31
00:02:05,770 --> 00:02:10,464
We load the seeds with dbt seed and look at the table in the SQL editor.

32
00:02:10,744 --> 00:02:13,496
Seven payment types, built from a file in git.
